   6: 
   7: ### 4.6 A worked white-dwarf calculation: retarded scattering memory and orbital-timescale bounds
   8: 
   9: **Imported from prior work.** Spectroscopy gives the inner white dwarf of PSR J0337+1715 \(T_{\rm eff}=15{,}800\pm100\) K, \(\log g=5.82\pm0.05\), \(R=0.091\pm0.005\,R_\odot\) and a hydrogen (DA) atmosphere \cite{kaplan2014j0337}. Masses and semimajor axes below are those of the Section 5.9 parameter set (inner white dwarf \(0.19754\,M_\odot\)). Orbital eccentricities are from \cite{ransom2014triple}.
  10: 
  11: **Counterexample candidate.** We examine one internal state of this carrier: the scalar-driven displacement of its own matter. The notation below is local to this section and avoids the symbols of Sections 3–5.
  12: - The declared scalar-tensor coupling is \(A(\varphi)=e^{\beta_s\varphi^2/2}\) with \(\beta_s=-4\), so the matter coupling is \(\alpha_s(\varphi)=\beta_s\varphi\).
  13: - As in Section 4.3, \(a_i\) and \(a_o\) are the charges per unit mass of the inner and outer white dwarfs, and \(a_p\) is the pulsar's.
  14: - The static metric is \(ds^2=-a^2c^2dt^2+B^2dr^2+r^2d\Omega^2\). The optical coordinate is \(x=-\int_r^{R_d}(B/a)\,dr\), and \(\Psi=r\,\delta\varphi\).
  15: - \(M_i\) is the white dwarf's mass and \(m_i=GM_i/c^2\) its geometric mass.
  16: 
  17: The background is a static weak-field model with a helium core and a hydrogen-rich envelope (\(X\simeq0.89\)). It has \(M_i=0.198\,M_\odot\), \(R=6.91\times10^9\) cm, \(T_{\rm eff}=15{,}898\) K and \(\log g=5.740\), with a gray Eddington envelope built with Rosseland tables. Its scalar profile \(\varphi_0(r)\) has asymptotic value \(\varphi_\infty=10^{-3}\), which is a linearization point.
  18: 
  19: The drive is an ingoing pulse \(\Psi_{\rm in}=\eta R_d\,\mathcal P((t+x/c)/t_p)\), where \(\mathcal P(s)=(4s(1-s))^4\) on \(0<s<1\) and zero otherwise, with \(t_p=1.717\) ms, \(\eta=10^{-30}\) and \(R_d=6.91\times10^9\) cm. It drives the coupled evolution of baryons, gas, photon transport and one returned GR metric until \(t_e=2t_p\).
  20: 
  21: The readout is \(q=-\Psi_{\rm out}/m_i\) at \(R_d\), the change of \(a_i\) seen in the exterior monopole. It excludes the instantaneous polarization \(\beta_sT_0\delta\varphi\), which belongs to the wave operator; in the coupled calculation that polarization produces a Born scattering of \(1.7\times10^{-10}\) of the incident field. The pulse length \(ct_p\simeq515\) km is far below \(R\), so this is a finite-size response outside the point-particle regime \(\lambda\gg R\).
  22: 
  23: **Imported from prior work.** At the protocol endpoint the readout is negative. We extrapolate interior refinements by 1, 2 and 4 with the Richardson method (observed order 2.00, change from 2\(\times\) to 4\(\times\) of \(-1.2\%\)). We then apply a mass-normalization factor \(1+2.2\times10^{-5}\) measured on the unrefined solution. The result is
  24: 
  25: \begin{equation}
  26: q(t_e)=-2.3\times10^{-51},\qquad q(t_e)/(\eta\varphi_\infty^2)\simeq-2.3\times10^{-15}.
  27: \label{eq:wd-readout}
  28: \end{equation}
  29: 
  30: The corresponding outgoing amplitude is about \(10^{-26}\) of the incident field, so at \(t_e\) the static polarization dominates the exterior monopole. The readout is tied to the protocol: it rises steeply near \(t_e\), to \(-2.8\times10^{-51}\) at 3.5 ms and \(-8.7\times10^{-51}\) at 4.0 ms.
  31: - The instantaneous product of the pulse and the background geometry contributes \(10^{-8}\) of the readout.
  32: - The rest-mass redistribution of baryons contributes 0.999996, and 99.9% of the readout comes from layers the pulse has already left.
  33: - With the background fixed, disabling the Planck–Larkin occupation probabilities in the equation of state and opacities changes the readout by at most a relative \(2.2\times10^{-10}\).
  34: 
  35: **Proven.** For pressureless matter at linear order, the radial displacement
  36: 
  37: \begin{equation}
  38: \xi=4c^2\frac{a^2}{B^2}\eta R_dt_p^2\Big[\Big(\frac{\varphi_0'}{r}-\frac{\varphi_0}{r^2}\Big)F_2(p)+\frac{\varphi_0x'}{rct_p}F_1(p)\Big],\qquad p=\frac{t+x/c}{t_p},
  39: \label{eq:free-fall}
  40: \end{equation}
  41: 
  42: with \(F_1=\int_0^p\mathcal P\) and \(F_2=\int_0^pF_1\), solves \(\ddot\xi=-c^2(a^2/B^2)\partial_r[\alpha_s(\varphi_0)\delta\varphi]\) with \(\xi=\dot\xi=0\) before the pulse arrives. **Imported from prior work.** The resulting face mass fluxes \(4\pi r^2B\rho_0\xi\), passed through the retarded propagator of the coupled calculation, reproduce its continuum limit to \(5.2\times10^{-5}\).
