  51: whose static limit is \(\sum_ew_e\delta M_e/M_i\). A mass-conserving redistribution therefore contributes only in two ways: through the radial variation of \(w\), and through light-crossing windows that are not yet complete.
  52: 
  53: **Imported from prior work.** At \(t_e\) the static-window value is \(+3.6\times10^{-54}\). It has the opposite sign to the readout and 1/640 of its magnitude. The endpoint readout is thus the retarded monopole of a transient redistribution, Equation (\ref{eq:wd-window}), not a static charge of the displaced configuration.
  54: 
  55: **Imported from prior work.** We also evolved a whole-star linear adiabatic radial model with Newtonian self-gravity. It uses the force of Equation (\ref{eq:free-fall}) and the regular pulse reflected through the centre. It reproduces the endpoint readout to \(-0.81\%\) and the free-fall displacement to \(1.2\times10^{-6}\). In source time the pulse reaches the centre at 0.2305 s and leaves the star at 0.4627 s. In readout time \(t_R\) at \(R_d\), the history is as follows.
  56: - The readout stays negative until \(t_R\simeq0.43\) s. It grows from \(-2.3\times10^{-51}\) at \(t_e\) to \(-3.0\times10^{-43}\) at 0.1 s, \(-8.2\times10^{-41}\) at 0.23 s and \(-2.7\times10^{-39}\) at 0.35 s, as denser layers are displaced.
  57: - The signals from the centre arrival and from the outgoing pass reach the readout together near \(2R_d/c\simeq0.46\) s. There \(|q|\) is largest, \(3.5\times10^{-36}\), an outgoing amplitude \(1.5\times10^{-11}\) of the incident one.
  58: - After the pulse has left, the star rings in radial p modes, with dominant periods of 44–59 s and an rms readout of \(8.1\times10^{-45}\).
  59: 
  60: Only the endpoint is cross-checked against the coupled calculation. The model is linear, adiabatic and Newtonian. The photosphere, whose thermal times are at most 0.4 s, is not adiabatic during the ringing.
  61: 
  62: **Proven.** In a discrete linear system with positive-definite stiffness, the response after a force of finite duration is a finite sum of nonzero-frequency modes. Its time average vanishes, and it tends to zero if every mode is positively damped. **Imported from prior work.** The radial model's lowest eigenvalue is \(\omega_0^2=1.25\times10^{-3}\) s\(^{-2}\), a period of 178 s. **Conjectural.** Assume the star's radial modes are damped; we made no non-adiabatic stability calculation. Then the pulse-induced change of \(a_i\) vanishes at first order in \(\eta\), as for any stable linear response. A change of order \(\eta^2\) from absorbed energy remains.
  63: 
  64: The endpoint value is subject to the following corrections.
  65: - **Proven.** In the Newtonian radial channel, self-gravity feedback changes the displacement over \(t_e\) by at most a fraction \(2\pi G\rho_{\max}t_e^2=4.5\times10^{-18}\). **Conjectural.** With post-Newtonian terms neglected, the once-returned GR solution differs from the self-consistent one at this order.
  66: - **Conjectural.** Curvature backscatter changes the flat-space readout at order \(m_i/R_d=4.2\times10^{-6}\). This is an order estimate, not a bound.
  67: - **Proven.** Because \(\alpha_s\) is linear in \(\varphi\), the second-order force and source terms are at most \(|\delta\varphi|/\varphi_0=1.0\times10^{-27}\) of the first-order ones. This compares those terms only; it does not bound the full readout error.
  68: - **Proven.** Invariance under \(\varphi\to-\varphi\) makes \(q/\eta\), at first order in \(\eta\), even in \(\varphi_\infty\). **Conjectural.** The force and the readout weight are both proportional to \(\alpha_s(\varphi_0)\), so the dynamic part scales as \(\varphi_\infty^2\) at leading order in the weak background field. The exponent of the \(10^{-8}\) direct part is not fixed.
  69: - **Imported from prior work.** The ADM mass differs from the mass at the readout radius by a relative \(6.8\times10^{-11}\).
  70: 
  71: **Imported from prior work.** The magnitude of the readout is set by the photosphere.
  72: - The kernel centroid lies 363 km below the \(P_{\rm gas}=1\) dyn cm\(^{-2}\) cut, at gray optical depth 1.06. The kernel is nearly single-signed (sign margin 0.9988), so no positive density profile reverses the sign.
  73: - A spherical gray envelope rebuilt with the same equation of state and tables reproduces the declared density with a kernel-weighted deviation of 0.25% (maximum 0.38%).
  74: - At fixed mass, and with the pulse kernel of the declared geometry, varying \(\log g\) or \(T_{\rm eff}\) alone within Kaplan's 1\(\sigma\) ranges scales \(|q(t_e)|\) in Equation (\ref{eq:wd-readout}) by 1.6–6.2, and by 3.2 at the central values.
  75: - An LTE non-gray correction multiplies this by a further 1.7–2.8 (2.1 at the central values). It uses H and He continuum opacity whose Rosseland mean is 0.55–0.74 of the tables, is plane-parallel, and is converged to \(5.6\times10^{-5}\) in flux for \(\tau_R\le100\).
  76: 
  77: **Conjectural.** These magnitudes only show how strongly the protocol readout depends on surface gravity; no conclusion below uses them.
  78: 
  79: **Conjectural.** At orbital timescales, the white dwarf's restoring times are far shorter than the lags \(\tau_\chi=2\)–500 d of Section 5.
  80: - **Radial.** The lowest radial mode has a period of 178 s, and the acoustic cutoff periods of the charge layers are 28–47 s. At the inner orbit the in-phase correction is \((\omega/\omega_0)^2=1.6\times10^{-6}\). At equal amplitude, a drive longer than the star exerts a monopole force about \(3\times10^{-8}\) of the pulse's through the \(\varphi_0'\) term. Including the change of the self-coupling raises this to order \(10^{-7}\).
  81: - **Tidal.** **Proven.** On a static spherical background, linear response couples the monopole \(a_i\) only to the \(l=0\) part of the drive, so tides do not enter it at linear order. The equilibrium tide sets the size of the tide itself: the apsidal constant is \(k_2=3.3\times10^{-4}\), the relative tidal force is \(9.1\times10^{-12}\), and the tidal apsidal rate is \(1.5\times10^{-5}\) of the 1PN rate. The dynamical tide is dissipative. A radiative damping-depth estimate (\(8.9\times10^6\)) uses a weak-damping formula outside its validity, so the absence of discrete g-mode resonances is not established.
  82: - **Dissipative.** The adiabatic static structural susceptibility is the change of \(a_i\) per uniform scalar shift from hydrostatic readjustment. It is \(|\chi_{\rm struct}|\le8.8\times10^{-9}\) at \(\varphi_\infty=10^{-3}\), scaling as \(\varphi_\infty^2\), which is \(2.2\times10^{-9}\) of \(|\beta_s|\). Deeper layers do have thermal times comparable to the orbital period. For a single-relaxation (Debye) response, the lagged part is at most half of the relaxing static part. Resonant or strongly overdamped internal dynamics fall outside this bound. Within this bound, the result supports holding the white-dwarf charges fixed in Section 4.3.
  83: 
  84: **Conjectural.** For J0337 we give only a magnitude comparison. A change \(\delta a_i\) modulates the pulsar–inner pair factor by \(a_p\,\delta a_i\) and the inner–outer pair factor by \(a_o\,\delta a_i\), and leaves the pulsar–outer pair unchanged. This is not the common modulation of the Section 4.3 template, so the coefficient envelopes of Table 1 do not translate into it directly (Section 5.4). For the free-fall mechanism,
  85: 
  86: \begin{equation}
  87: |\delta a_i|\le|\chi_{\rm struct}|\,\delta\varphi_{\rm mod},\qquad \delta\varphi_{\rm mod}\le3.1\times10^{-10}|a_p|+2.0\times10^{-10}|a_o|,
  88: \label{eq:wd-modulation}
  89: \end{equation}
  90: 
  91: where \(\delta\varphi_{\rm mod}\) is the orbital modulation of the companions' scalar field at the white dwarf. The first term comes from the inner eccentricity; the second from the outer eccentricity and the inner-orbit motion. The Cassini measurement \cite{bertotti2003cassini} with \(\gamma-1=-2\alpha_0^2/(1+\alpha_0^2)\) \cite{damour1992tensor}, taken at its 2\(\sigma\) lower limit, gives \(|\alpha_0|\le3.5\times10^{-3}\), that is \(\varphi_\infty\le8.8\times10^{-4}\) for \(\beta_s=-4\). The declared \(\varphi_\infty=10^{-3}\) lies outside this range, so we rescale \(\chi_{\rm struct}\) as \(\varphi_\infty^2\). With \(|a_o|\simeq|\alpha_0|\) and \(|a_p|\le1\), the pulsar–inner modulation is at most \(2.2\times10^{-18}\), and the inner–outer modulation at most \(7.6\times10^{-21}\). Both are at least eight orders of magnitude below every stored Section 5 scale at 2 d. Those scales run from \(2.8\times10^{-10}\) (K=1, truncated) through \(4.1\times10^{-10}\) (physical-drive endpoint), \(1.7\times10^{-9}\) (K=10, truncated) and \(3.5\times10^{-9}\) (K=10, full) to \(1.57\times10^{-7}\) (K\(\approx\)934). The computed free-fall state is therefore not a candidate carrier for a lag at that sensitivity. Other internal states of either white dwarf, and the neutron-star charge of Section 4.3, are not addressed.
  92: 
  93: **Conjectural.** In summary, a scalar pulse leaves in the displaced matter of the inner white dwarf a transient retarded monopole signal with no first-order permanent charge. At orbital timescales this state couples to the monopole charge only through static coefficients and a bounded structural susceptibility. It therefore does not supply the orbital-timescale internal state that the Section 3 benchmark requires; the repository ledger names the absence of such a state in the free-fall sector assumption A4. The remaining assumptions are:
  94: - linear response;
  95: - a Newtonian radial model with a flat-exterior readout;
  96: - positive damping of the radial modes;
  97: - neglect of interior Born scattering, where the core compactness is about \(10^{-4}\);
  98: - a single-relaxation bound for the lag;
  99: - the unfixed \(\varphi_\infty\) exponent of the \(10^{-8}\) direct part;
 100: - a non-gray correction with simplified LTE continuum opacity and a fixed pulse kernel;
 101: - a slowly rotating white dwarf;
 102: - a mixed hydrogen–helium envelope, whereas the observed star has a hydrogen (DA) atmosphere \cite{kaplan2014j0337}.
 103: 
